  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1236 PM CDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nLow pressure continues to track east northeast into western IA \nthrough this afternoon. Scattered showers and isolated thunderstorms \nwill be possible with this feature. The main effects should be this \nafternoon and confined to areas near and south of highway 20 in \nnorthwest IA. With PWAT values around 1.75\" to 2\", locally heavy \nrain will be possible, especially near this low pressure along and \nsouth of highway 20. Some question marks surround the air mass in \nWoodbury and Ida counties, locations that have received the most \nrainfall the past two days, as the morning stratiform rainfall seems \nto be providing some stability to the area and suppressing stronger \nconvective activity. The latest Hi-res models continue to trend away \nfrom heavier rain near highway 20, with mainly spotty activity \nexpected. A few downpours could lead to very localized amounts \naround an inch or so, but flash flooding is unlikely. Farther \nnorthwest, spotty showers will be possible with weak, diurnally \ndriven instability. The overall effects of these diurnally driven \nshowers will be low.\n\nSaturday will likely start with stratus and fog in many locations. A \nweak wave will move through the area and likely lead to spotty \nshowers and thunderstorms. Some hints that the better instability \n(about 1000 J/kg) will be north of I-90, but overall weak shear and \n13 kft freezing levels suggest that pockets of heavy rain will be \nthe main threat.\n\nBy Saturday night this wave will move southeast of the area, leaving \nmainly dry conditions in place for much of Saturday night into \nSunday morning. Model soundings hint at the potential for some \nmorning fog, but the depth of the low level moist layer looks much \nshallower, so a better chance to burn off in the morning. Drier \nnortherly flow should lead to a pleasant Sunday with highs of 75 to \n80 and dew points likely in the lower to mid 60s for most locations.\n\nSunday night into Monday should see mostly dry and warm conditions \ncontinue. Monday night into Tuesday night will be the next better \nchance for showers and storms as a trough digs in to the north and \nnortheast. For now, the threat for severe storms and heavy rain \nlooks low, but this will be a time frame to watch for changes.\n\nCooler and more stable air should settle in behind this system, with \nhighs mostly in the 70s Thursday and Friday and only isolated \nchances for showers and storms.",
